  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Principle
    
    A refinement of Slide Prism — the aurora-light technique for moving objects through crystalline media — the Mechanism Refine compresses kinetic force into crystalline matrices, storing a hammer-strike's worth of power inside a fist-sized conductor for surgical release on command. Where Slide Prism displaces, Refine accumulates. The technique demands absolute stillness during charging; force must be layered incrementally, and constant attunement maintained throughout. A moment's inattention shatters the crystal, taking bone with it.
    
    ## Origin and Formalization
    
    Developed in The Promenade Ward during the early Frozen Peace by practitioners of Qingfu'spire, Aurora Stack's governing council, who needed construction methods faster than conventional labor could provide. Qingoqa aihua'Mist-wrought, Qingfu'spire's festival herald and crystal-worker, is credited as the first to master and teach the technique.
    
    Technique-patterns resembling the Refine have been found preserved in The Scared~ Shroud, a fire-core tome housed in the Nightshelf's deep vaults, suggesting pre-Faction Wars antecedents. Whether Qingfu'spire formalized an existing practice or independently derived it remains unresolved.
    
    ## Practitioners and Applications
    
    The Refine spread rapidly across Aurora Stack factions: the Pinnacle (the shadowice-bound splinter group), the Qingqe'frostlings (who adapted it for tunnel-work), and individual practitioners including the faceless Xi and Beacon-Keeper Lianhinglou. Grand Auditor Mingmeyang, founder of Promenade Hei, and Zhinghoua lang'Gleam are also listed among its practitioners. Primary applications include sealing fractures in fire-cores, repairing damaged aurora-crystals, and structural construction — extending the life of trade materials that would otherwise be lost.
    
    ## Cost
    
    The technique's physical toll accumulates rather than announces itself. Documented injuries include progressive flipper numbness after sustained daily use and — most severely — crystal fracture during calibration, which drives shards into the practitioner's body. Beacon-Keeper Lianhinglou lost sight in one eye to such a fracture. Lianhinglou still practices. Practitioners glow faintly during calibration, their internal light flickering as they work — a visible marker of the attunement the technique demands, and a reminder that what glows from within is also what breaks from within.
    
    The technique spread anyway. The alternative was slower.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
